// The flitter's body. This file exists for the identical reason
// `hopper.ron` above it does, and the wiring audit's own doc names the
// failure a missing copy of this file produces: `place_creature` resolves
// an animal's material by its species' own name
// (`world.materials.id_of("flitter")`), so without it the species loads,
// compiles, sits on the COLONY chip -- and places zero animals. `hopper.ron`
// shipped exactly one file short of hatching once already; this ships with
// its material in the same change.
//
// Same physics as `hopper.ron`'s copy of the ant's: a creature cell the
// sweep never moves, flammable on the same terms, live flesh worth the same
// to whatever eats it (`food_energy`/`food_class` copied unchanged -- this
// file was not asked to reprice what a flitter is worth dead, only what it
// looks like alive).
//
// **The colour is the one place this file departs, on purpose and by
// design.** `Reports/evolution-lab-pollinator-design-2026-09-10.md` §2.7:
// a pale cool blue-white -- (196,214,236) / (224,236,250) / (160,180,208).
// Soil is dark brown, plants are green, ants are near-black, and the hopper
// already took warm ochre; this is the hue the box's palette has no other
// user for, and a pale body stays legible in flight, which is where this
// animal is looked at. **Deliberately not a flower colour** -- `flower.ron`
// carries eight bands running yellow through white, and a pollinator that
// matched its own food would vanish at the exact moment (arriving at a
// bloom) a review card needs it to read.
(
    name: "flitter",
    display: "Flitter",
    kind: Creature,
    density: 1.0,
    colors: [
        (196, 214, 236),
        (224, 236, 250),
        (160, 180, 208),
    ],
    flammability: 0.6,
    burn_temperature: 500.0,
    burn_duration: 40,
    burns_into: "corpse",
    heat_conductivity: 0.1,
    food_energy: 480.0,
    penetration_resistance: 0.25,
    food_class: 1.0,
)
